    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d448">

                <pb facs="0562" n="304" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg304"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/567½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at London</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Skinner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 215]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-01-01">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 1, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received January 12.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Referring to the Department’s cabled
                    instruction of September 22<anchor n="2" xml:id="d448-f1"/> stating that it did
                    not consider the British Government to be entitled to collect freight on cargo
                    on diverted, detained, or seized vessels sailing before the war, and subsequent
                    correspondence dealing with the same point, I have the honor to enclose herewith
                    a copy of a note received from the Foreign Office, through the Ambassador, in
                    reply to my representations. I shall be greatly interested in the Department’s
                    opinion respecting the contents of this note.</p>
                <p>The Foreign Office states that payment of freight will be required in all cases
                    in which the cargo released “would have been condemned either to confiscation or
                    to detention if it had been taken into the prize court, but in which the neutral
                    shipper asks for its release to him.”</p>
                <p>I do not quite perceive how the authorities are able to determine in advance that
                    a given consignment would have been condemned had the matter gone to the prize
                    court, and in any case, I do not understand how the Foreign Office can waive to
                    one side the Department’s reiterated contention that goods that cleared before
                    the war are not subject to condemnation from any point of view.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert P. Skinner</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>



                <xi:include href="d448-a1.xml" xpointer="d448-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
